, keyword arguments, and any function not listed above.\nHow the result is computed: every node reports its return value plus Laplace noise scaled to the\nprogram\'s sensitivity (the most one changed edge can change all reports together), and the aggregate\nis an arithmetic expression over total (the sum of all node reports) and n. If one substructure is\ncounted by several nodes or several times, correct for it with constant factors.\nSmaller sensitivity means less noise: bound degrees with project(P, D) before nonlinear functions.\nReading D or dmax, or calling N, makes the system spend part of the budget on a noisy degree bound.\nHow to write common Python constructs in this language (S is any set expression, x any number):\n- unordered pairs of distinct elements of S, instead of sum(1 for v in S for w in S if v < w):\n  0.5 * sum(size(S) - 1 for v in S)\n- unordered pairs of elements of S that are neighbors in the noisy graph, instead of a condition\n  \'if v < w and w in N(v)\': 0.5 * sum(size(S & N(v)) for v in S)\n- unordered pairs of elements of S that are not neighbors in the noisy graph, instead of \'w not in N(v)\':\n  0.5 * sum(size(S - N(v)) - 1 for v in S)   (v itself is in S - N(v), hence the - 1)\n- in general, never test membership or order: count the matching elements with size(...) of a set\n  expression and correct repeated counting with a constant factor (0.5 for pairs counted in both orders)\n- x ** 2 is pow(x, 2); comb(x, k) and pow(x, k) need k written as a number\n- a running total in a for loop is sum(EXPR for v in S); a name is assigned once (use a new name per step)\n- this node is self; other nodes exist only as loop variables of sum(...)\nPer-node architecture (this run):\n- D is this node\'s own public degree bound (each node has its own); project(P, D) keeps at most D neighbors.\n- A(x, y): public unbiased estimate of the edge between nodes x and y, computed from randomized-response\n  bits of the projected neighbor lists: E[A(x, y)] = 1 if the edge is kept, 0 otherwise; A(x, x) = 0.\n  Each value is noisy (between -1/(e^eps1 - 1) and e^eps1/(e^eps1 - 1)) but sums of A are unbiased counts.\n  A program reads either N(...) or A(...), not both; reading A costs the same budget as N.\n\nThis design round: do not use N(...). If the statistic needs edges between other nodes, read the unbiased matrix A(...); if it does not, use P only.\n\nThe input prompt from the analyst is:\n"""\nFor each node, the number of 2-stars centered at that node is the number of unordered pairs of its neighbors. This can be calculated as degree(node) * (degree(node) - 1) / 2, where degree(node) is the number of edges connected to the node. The total number of 2-stars in the graph is the sum of 2-stars centered at each node. Each node may hide its edge information under the local edge differential privacy framework. Q: The nodes are numbered from 0 to 48. What is the total number of 2-stars in the graph?\n"""\n\nThe graph has 49 nodes.\n\nYour previous attempts were rejected:\n\nAttempt 1:\n```dsl\ndegree = size(P)\nreturn (degree * (degree - 1)) / 2\n```\naggregate: total\nResult: rejected: sensitivity GS = 47 is too loose: it grows with the number of nodes (GS = 194 on a graph with 196 nodes and the same degree bound), so a set in the program is bounded only by n; bound the neighbor sets with project(..., D) before counting\n\nWrite a corrected program. Do not repeat a rejected program.\n\nBefore you answer, check the program line by line:\n1. no if, in, not in, <, >, == and no {...} or [...] anywhere;\n2. no **, // or %;\n3. every argument of comb, pow, + - * / is a number: size(S), never a set S;\n4. the only node names are self and the loop variables of sum(...);\n5. every name is assigned once and the last line is the return line.\n\nReply with exactly two parts after your reasoning:\n1. one code block that opens with the line ```dsl and closes with the line ```, holding only the program lines (no tags, no aggregate line inside the block);\n2. after the block, one line that starts with \'aggregate:\' followed by an arithmetic expression over total and n.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.6, top_p=0.95, top_k=20, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=13000, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: None, lora_request: None, prompt_adapter_request: None.
